== 2017 -- 2021

I got my *Bachelor's degree in Computer Science and Economics* ("Wirtschaftsinformatik" in german) from the #link("https://www.nordakademie.de/en/")[Nordakademie], a private university near Hamburg. While studying I was a trainee at #link("https://www.cancom.com/")[CANCOM], which provides IT hardware and services to local businesses. During this phase, I was trained in all levels of IT (from building servers to developing software). I found my love for programming while building specialized monitoring tools for our Security Operations Center.

== 2021 -- 2025
After my Bachelor's time, I transferred to #link("https://justtrack.io/")[justtrack] where I worked as #link("https://go.dev/")[Go] developer doing agile development of digital Ad-tracking and analytics products. I learned a lot about software development at scale, navigating millions of lines of code and building services that process thousands of events per second. 

In 2023 I started a *Master's degree in Bioinformatics* at the #link("https://www.uni-hamburg.de/en.html")[University of Hamburg] while I continued working as a part-time Software Developer. I finished the degree in 2025 with my thesis on "Low-Rank Representation of Two-Electron Integrals" in the #link("https://www.chemie.uni-hamburg.de/en/institute/ac/toelle.html")[Tölle Research Group].

== 2026 -- Now

After my Master's thesis I started a Ph.D. in the Tölle Research Group where I now work on developing novel ways to employ advanced Tensor Decomposition techniques (like Tensor Hypercontraction) in the study of highly correlated quantum materials. To this end I developed the open source Python library #link("https://github.com/QuantumCorrelators/pythc")[PyTHC], which implements a multitude of algorithms in the Least-Squares Tensor Hypercontraction framework and aims to improve accessibility and comparability in the LS-THC landscape. A preprint of the accompanying paper can be found under #link(url-base + "publication")[Publications].
